{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310537,"nonce":"CfRo1381bqFWRoebto+zS7Ap4iI=","title":"Every benchmark_runner invocation is a fresh process, so a single run always measures a cold Compile column","message":"`benchmark_runner` is a NEW PROCESS per invocation, so the FIRST run of any target\npays ~20-30ms of OS page-cache cold start that lands entirely in the **Compile**\ncolumn. Execute is unaffected — it stays rock-steady — which is the tell:\n**variance confined to Compile = cold start; variance in both = machine load.**\n\nMeasured at 68dbcda1, fibonacci / rayzor-tiered, quiet machine:\n\n    rep1 compile=23.15ms   execute=330.01ms   \u003c- cold\n    rep2 compile= 4.24ms   execute=329.58ms\n    rep3 compile= 4.43ms   execute=329.34ms\n    rep4 compile= 4.03ms   execute=327.81ms\n    rep5 compile= 3.94ms   execute=329.08ms\n\n**This produced an INVERTED conclusion, not just noise.** Comparing single cold runs\nof fibonacci (452B src) and nbody (4611B src) gave 37.15ms vs 33.68ms — reading as\n\"compile cost is FIXED, so it must be stdlib work, so pre-optimise the snapshot\".\nWarm + interleaved (discard one warmup per benchmark, alternate arms, 3 reps):\n\n    fibonacci: 4.08 / 3.96 / 3.71  (~3.9ms)\n    nbody:    14.99 / 16.31 / 14.51 (~15.3ms)\n\nCompile SCALES with the program's own code (~4x for ~10x source). The \"fixed cost\"\nwas the cold-start penalty swamping an 11ms real difference.\n\n**RULE: state which regime you are measuring, and interleave arms either way.**\n- Comparing TWO BUILDS or two programs =\u003e warm + interleaved, or cold-start swamps\n  the difference (it inverted the fibonacci/nbody comparison above).\n- Judging what a USER PAYS =\u003e **cold is the product, not contamination.** `rayzor run\n  foo.hx` is a fresh process every time; the warm steady state only exists inside a\n  benchmark loop. Discarding warmups optimises for a regime real invocations never see.\n\nDo not compare a number taken today against one taken earlier in a session without\nre-measuring both in the SAME regime.\n\nConsequence for pre-optimising stdlib MIR in `snapshot-gen`: **the warm numbers do NOT\nsettle it.** Warm, fibonacci's entire compile is 3.9ms so the stdlib O2 share is small —\nbut cold is the regime that matters for startup, and cold adds ~20-30ms whose breakdown\nis UNMEASURED (page-cache faulting vs snapshot decode vs O2 over stdlib MIR are not yet\nseparated). `snapshot-gen/src/main.rs` does `load_stdlib` + `lower_to_tast` with NO\nPassManager, so the carried stdlib is lowered-but-unoptimised and O2 genuinely does run\nover it per-exec — which is precisely the \"setup work redone per-exec\" shape\n[[feedback-snapshot-is-setup-phase-map-dont-build]] names. NEEDS a cold breakdown before\naccepting or rejecting.\n\nSee [[feedback-perf-measurement-protocol]], [[feedback-interleave-ab-arms-not-more-reps]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_benchmark_runner_cold_start_contaminates_compile` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}